clearQueue: () => void;

/**
Returns a promise that resolves when the limiter is idle.

The limiter is idle when there are no promises currently running (including reserved slots) and no promises waiting to run in the queue.

Calling this method when the limiter is already idle resolves normally.

Unlike `clearQueue()`, pending promises are not discarded; the returned promise simply waits for all of them to settle.
*/
onIdle: () => Promise<void>;

### limit.clearQueue()

Discard pending promises that are waiting to run.

This might be useful if you want to tear down the queue at the end of your program's lifecycle or discard any function calls referencing an intermediary state of your app.

Note: This does not cancel promises that are already running.

When `rejectOnClear` is enabled, pending promises are rejected with an `AbortError`.
This is recommended if you await the returned promises, for example with `Promise.all`, so pending tasks do not remain unresolved after `clearQueue()`.

### limit.onIdle()

Returns a promise that resolves when the limiter is idle.

The limiter is idle when there are no promises currently running (including reserved slots) and no promises waiting to run in the queue.

Calling this method when the limiter is already idle resolves normally.

Unlike `clearQueue()`, this does not discard pending promises; the returned promise only settles once all of them have finished. This is useful to wait for all in-flight work, for example before shutting down.

```js
import pLimit from 'p-limit';

const limit = pLimit(2);

limit(() => doSomething());
limit(() => doSomethingElse());

await limit.onIdle();
console.log('All work finished');
```

